    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3323.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 786,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical States (The Three Phases)\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The state we drink, swim in, and use for rain (found in rivers, lakes, and oceans).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water (found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and polar caps).\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Water in the air (found in humidity, clouds, and steam from a boiling kettle).\n\n### 2. Unique and Miraculous Properties\nScientists consider water to be a \"weird\" liquid because it defies many standard rules of chemistry. These anomalies are precisely what make life possible:\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a transport system, carrying vital chemicals, minerals, and nutrients in living things and in rivers and oceans.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This means ice floats on top of water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, killing all marine life and preventing life on Earth.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees against gravity).\n\n### 3. Water and Life\n*   **The Blueprint for Biology:** Life as we know it began in water, and all living organisms require it. The human body is made up of about 60% water; our brains are about 73% water. \n*   **Metabolism:** Without water, the chemical reactions that keep us alive (like digestion and cellular function) cannot take place.\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth’s surface** is covered by water, mostly in the oceans (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n*   **The Scarcity:** Despite the planet looking so blue, **only about 2.5% of Earth's water is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use in lakes and rivers.\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly recycled through the hydrologic cycle: it evaporates from the oceans into the atmosphere, forms clouds, falls back to Earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back into the oceans via rivers. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### Summary\nWater is the lifeblood of planet Earth. It shapes our geography, regulates our climate, drives our weather, and makes up the majority of our bodies. Without it, life as we know it would simply not exist."
